implementationPlan.description:
DRAIN AT THE SOURCE: FIX THE HELPER, NOT THE CALL SITE (live-goal-release.test.tsx).

Make `answerCode` at :209-211 async, drain first, then read:

  const answerCode = async (): Promise<string> => {
    await settle();
    return screen.getByTestId("cr.release.answer").querySelector("code")?.textContent ?? "";
  };

and `await` it at all four call sites — :232, :239, :284, :295. Update the helper's doc comment to say WHY the drain is there: the submit is dispatched fire-and-forget from `decide()`, so `act` around the click does not await it, and this read must flush the queue before looking.

USE THE FILE'S OWN `settle()` (:130-132, `await act(async () => { await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(ms); })`). `advanceTimersByTimeAsync` flushes the microtask queue between ticks, which is exactly the guarantee missing at :211, and the arm at :252 already proves it works on this component: it calls `confirmRelease()`, then `await settle(5_000)`, and reads state reliably. This is deterministic — not "more likely to pass".

DO NOT REACH FOR `await screen.findByTestId(...)`, which the filing suggests, unless you first MEASURE that it works here. This file arms fake timers in `beforeEach` at :33 for EVERY arm, and no file in this repo runs RTL's async utilities under an active fake clock: the only two control-room files that use both (live-runs.test.tsx, prd-coverage.test.tsx) scope `vi.useFakeTimers()` inside one `it` with a `finally { vi.useRealTimers(); }`, and their `findBy`/`waitFor` arms are separate arms under real timers. `waitFor` polls on `setInterval`; if RTL does not detect vitest's fake clock it never advances and the arm HANGS to the vitest timeout — a slow red is worse for this lane than the fast flake you are removing.

DO NOT put the drain inside `confirmRelease()`. It would cover the same reads but also change the timing of the arm at :148, which flips the read to ABORT at :163 in the window that opens the moment `confirmRelease()` returns.

DO NOT TOUCH THE PRODUCTION COMPONENT in this step. `decide()` being fire-and-forget is deliberate — a click handler must not return a promise — and DoD 3 forbids editing the component to make a test pass unless a defect is actually measured. None is.

implementationPlan.note:
DRAINED AT THE SOURCE. TEST-ONLY EDIT. One file: `apps/control-room/src/v2/goals/live-goal-release.test.tsx`. The production component was NOT touched.

WHAT SHIPPED. `answerCode` is now `async (): Promise<string>`, drains before reading, and is `await`ed at ALL FOUR call sites (now :261, :268, :313, :324 after the doc comment grew). :313 is the site the filing reported; :261 is the identical unguarded shape it did not report.

ONE DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE STEP'S LITERAL SNIPPET, and it is a strengthening, not a substitution. The step prescribed a single unconditional `await settle()`. I shipped a BOUNDED DRAIN LOOP over the same primitive:

  const ANSWER_DRAINS = 10;
  const answerCode = async (): Promise<string> => {
    for (let drained = 0; drained < ANSWER_DRAINS; drained += 1) {
      if (screen.queryByTestId("cr.release.answer") !== null) break;
      await settle();
    }
    return screen.getByTestId("cr.release.answer").querySelector("code")?.textContent ?? "";
  };

WHY, and it rests on the step-1 measurement the plan did not have. The plan's rationale was "advanceTimersByTimeAsync flushes the MICROTASK queue". But the boundary this arm actually waits on is `crypto.subtle.digest` inside `spendOffer` (offer-wire.ts:97), which is threadpool-resolved: my probe showed it OUTLAST 50 chained `await null` hops at ~2.9ms wall. A single event-loop yield is therefore not provably sufficient under arbitrary load, and this row's whole defect is that "usually enough" is not enough. The loop waits for the landing rather than betting one turn on it. It uses the file's OWN `settle()` exactly as prescribed and adds no import.

EVERY GUARD THE STEP ASKED FOR IS PRESERVED:
- Fails FAST, not slow. The loop is bounded at 10; if the answer never arrives it falls through to `getByTestId`, which throws RTL's own "unable to find an element" error. It cannot hang to the vitest timeout. This is the specific trap the step warned `findByTestId` would set, and I did not use `findByTestId`.
- Costs nothing when there is nothing to wait for. The guard is checked FIRST, so the two already-covered call sites (:268 after `settle(20_000)`, :324 after `settle(TRANSPORT_BOUND_MS)`) consume ZERO drains and behave exactly as before.
- `settle()` advances 0ms, so looking at the answer never fires an arm's 5s read poll as a side effect.
- The drain is NOT in `confirmRelease()`, per the step: the arm at :148 that flips the read to ABORT right after that call returns is untouched.

DOC COMMENT REWRITTEN to say WHY, naming the fire-and-forget dispatch, the `crypto.subtle.digest` boundary with the measured 50-hop/~3ms figure, why the bound exists, and why `findByTestId` was rejected — so the next reader cannot "simplify" it back into the flake.

VERIFICATION RUN THIS STEP:
1. `npx vitest run src/v2/goals/live-goal-release.test.tsx --reporter=verbose` => EXIT 0, `Test Files 1 passed (1)` / `Tests 4 passed (4)`; all four arms named individually at 40/12/7/17ms.
2. `pnpm --filter @moe/control-room typecheck` => EXIT 0.
3. THAT TYPECHECK IS PROVEN NON-VACUOUS, not assumed: `npx tsc --project tsconfig.json --listFiles --noEmit | grep live-goal-release` returns BOTH `live-goal-release.tsx` and `live-goal-release.test.tsx`, so the edited file was genuinely in the program.

HONEST LIMIT ON THIS STEP'S EVIDENCE: on an idle host the answer is already present, so the loop body never executes and this green run does not by itself prove the drain does anything. That is precisely what step 3's counterfactual must establish, and I will not claim it here.

Peer dirt in the same directory, untouched: approve-plan-reject.test.tsx, approve-plan.test.tsx, plan-run-resolution.test.tsx.
